stream
6. After heaflng the arguments of the complainants, it is pertinent tonote that the respondent has failed to comply with the ordersissued by the MahaRERA authority to date and has not paid therefund along with the interest to the complainants as per thedirections issued in the said orders. Hence, the orders issued bythe MahaRERA authority remain not complied with by therespondent. Hence, any further time granted to the respondentwould lead to injustice to the complainants. Hence, thecomplainants are entitled to seek issuance of recovery warrantsagainst the respondent for recovery of the said amounts payableby the respondent.
7. Therefore, the MahaRERA has no alternative but to oroceed withaction under section 40(1) of RERA against the respondent. It istherefore, directed to issue individual recovery warrants undersection 40(1) of RERA read along with rule 3 of the MaharashtraReal Estate (Regulation & development) (Recovery of Interest,Penalty, Compensation, Fine Payable, Forms of Complaint &Appeals etc.) rules, 2017, against the booked flats in the saidproject of the respondent.
8. In view of the above, the 16 applications of non-comoliance standdisposed of.
Dr. Vasant Drabhu)
Page 5 of 5
Secretary, MahaRERA

endstream
